How to Start a Presentation: 7 Golden Openers to Wow any Audience in 2021 (+ Examples) - AhaSlides
The first moments of your presentation, no matter how brief, are a huge deal. They have a massive impact not only on what follows, but also whether or not your audience follows along with you.
AhaSlides lets you put interactive poll questions, brainstorms, word clouds and quizzes in your presentation for your audience to contribute to live, using just their phones.
Furthermore, have you ever stopped to wonder why an immediate question might be a great way to start a presentation?
Robert Kennedy III, international keynote speaker, lists 4 types of questions to use right at the beginning of your presentation:
– How often do you think about…?
(To be shown alongside something else) – How much do you agree with this statement?
– Why do you think so many people prefer this to this?
Imagination – What if you could…. ?
– Imagine if this happened. What would you do…?
– What’s your biggest fear?
You don’t ask them in the hope that your audience will stand up, one-by-one, and actually answer them.
Not only does opening in this way get your audience immediately paying attention, it also covers some of the other tips mentioned in this article. Including…
Making it visual – Their responses are presented in a visual graph, scale or word cloud.
Introduce Yourself as a Person, not a Presenter
He likens starting a presentation to meeting someone new at a bar. He’s not talking about quaffing 5 pints beforehand to establish some Dutch courage, more like introducing yourself in a way that feels friendly, natural and most of all, personal.
Hi, I’m Gary, I’ve been an economic biologist for 40 years and I want to talk to you about the microeconomics of ants.
No matter how interesting your topic is, no one wants to hear the far-too-commonly-used ‘name, title, topic’ procession, as it offers absolutely nothing personal to latch onto.
Oh hey, I’m Gary, I think we know someone in common…
This time, you’ve decided to treat your listener as a friend to be made, rather than as a passive audience. You’ve introduced yourself in a personal way that has made a connection and has opened the door to intrigue.
Think about how this magical sentence makes you feel:
For pretty much every child that hears these 4 words, this is an instant attention grabber. Even as a man in his 30s, this opener still sets my imagination whirring as to what might follow.
And they all involve people. Just like these:
“There’s a person at my company who once told me….”
Remember this Good stories are about people; they’re not about things. They’re not about products or companies or revenue, they’re about the lives, the achievements, the struggles and the sacrifices of the people behind the things.
Stories make YOU more relatable – Just like in tip #2, stories can make you, the presenter, seem more personal. Your own experiences with other people speak far louder to audiences than stale introductions of your topic.
They’re jargon busters – Ever heard a children’s story that starts with ‘once upon a time, Prince Charming drilled down on the actionability principle inherent in agile methodology‘?
Going virtual with your presentation?
Using a fact as an opener to a presentation is an instant attention grabber.
Here’s an example I recently used at an online event ran from Singapore
The speech I was giving was about our software, AhaSlides, which provides ways to make presentations and quizzes interactive without the need to use stacks of paper.
There’s a reason I chose the GIF above: it’s a mix between a fact and an engaging visual.
In fact, facts and visuals usually go hand-in-hand when it comes to how to start a presentation. Check out these facts about visuals:
It’s the last stat here that has the biggest implications for you.
I could spend all day telling you, through voice and text, about the impact of plastic in our oceans. You may not listen, but the chances are that you will be more convinced by a single image:
That’s because images, and art in particular, are way better at connecting to your emotions than I am. And connecting to emotions, whether it’s through introductions, stories, facts, quotes or images, is what gives a presentation its persuasive power.
What you really want is a quote that starts a presentation with a bang. To do that, it has to check these boxes:
Relevant: Something that helps you segue into your topic.
I’m not talking something completely heinous that gets you thrown out of the conference; just something that doesn’t encourage a unilateral ‘nod and move on’ response from your audience.
“When I was young I thought thatmoneywas the most important thing in life. Now that I am old, I know that it is” – Oscar Wilde
How many times have you, yourself, been an unwilling audience member in your 7th presentation of the day, needing some reason to smile as the presenter plunges you head-first into the 42 problems of stopgap solutioneering?
Aside from being a great stimulator, a bit of comedy can also give you these benefits:
If you are interested in joining a tech-driven team to take on big challenges in building a high-quality “made in Vietnam” product for the global market, while mastering the art of high-velocity development along the way, this position is for you.
Provide mentorship, guidance, coaching, and support to the junior and mid-level engineers in the team.
Ideally, you should have over 03 years of experience in Node.js, though it would also be OK if you come from a strong Python or Go background.
Having experience with VueJS or an equivalence would be a big advantage.
Having experience in team lead or management roles will be an advantage.
These 10 fun brainstorm activities for students are sure to change their opinions on group thinking.
#7: Nominal Group Storm
Don’t forget to check out our quick-guide and example questions for school brainstorm ideas!
These 5 classroom brainstorm activities are suited for individual brainstorming. Each student in the class submits their ideas before the whole class discusses all submitted ideas together.
Don’t worry, you’re not sending anyone to war in the Gulf with this student brainstorm activity.
The desert island question is great because it gives students free reign to think creatively. Some students might want items that help them escape the island, while others might want some home comforts to make a new life there.
Speaking of thinking creatively, here’s one of the most creative brainstorm activities for students, as it involves really thinking outside of the box.
Ideas can range from traditional to absolutely wild, but the point of the activity is to lean more on the wild side and encourage students to be completely free with their ideas.
Tip It’s best to give students an item that serves only one traditional use, like a face mask or a plant pot. The more restrictive the object’s function, the more creative the ideas will be.
This student brainstorm activity is based on the popular kids’ party game, Pass the Parcel.
Once time is up, play some music and get students to continually pass their paper around the circle. Once the music stops, students have a few minutes to read whichever paper they ended up with and add their own additions and critiques to the ideas in front of them.
Tip Encourage your students to focus more on additions than critiques. Additions are inherently more positive than critiques, and are much more likely to lead to great ideas.
Apologies for the crass title, but it was too big an opportunity to pass up.
It may just seem like a brainstorm ice breaker activity, or maybe a straight-up waste of time, but doing this actually frees creativity enormously. It’s fun, communal, and best of all, some of the ‘bad’ ideas’ may turn out to be diamonds in the rough.
This one starts by giving students a goal, reversing it to aim for the opposite goal, then reversing it back to figure out the solutions. Let’s take an example…
Students brainstorm the answers to this reverse question, maybe with answers like ‘make the presentation a total monologue’ and ‘take everyone’s phones away’.
Here are 5 brainstorm activities for students to complete in groups. Groups can vary depending on the size of your class, but it’s best to keep them to a maximum of 7 students if possible.
The teams’ jobs are to write down as many connections as possible between those two objects or concepts within a time limit. As always, ideas are encouraged to be as creative as possible.
Students don’t want to be seen offering ideas that get branded ‘stupid’ for fear of ridicule by classmates and low grades by the teacher.
Alternatively, in a live class setting, you can simply get all students to submit their ideas by writing them on a piece of paper and dropping them into a hat. You pick all the ideas out of the hat, write them on the board and give each idea a number.
Tip Anonymity can actually work wonders for classroom creativity. Try it with other activities like a live word cloud or a live quiz for students to get the most out of your class.
For many, this is one of the most engaging and fun brainstorm activities for students.
Let’s say, for example, that the topic is ‘how do we attract more visitors to the nautical history museum?
This is a great student brainstorm activity for getting participants to approach problems from a different point of view. Needless to say, this is a crucial skill to develop for tackling future problems, and even for developing empathy in general.
Start by separating your class into groups of about 5 or 6 participants. Announce the brainstorm topic to everyone, then ask all students except for 2 per group to leave the room.
