Teaching a new class, or getting reacquainted with one remotely, is never easy. Throw in the backdrop of the new normal, with all its online learning and hybrid classrooms, and you’re in at the deep end before you know it!
Where you always have: with getting to know your students.
It’s 100% free to download and use live with your students on interactive polling software!
If you need a quick refresher: a learning style is a student’s preferred method of learning.
Kinaesthetic – Learners who learn through actions. They prefer creating, building and playing.
This is a 25-question poll for you, the teacher, to give to your students in class. It has a variety of questions to test your students’ preferred learning styles and to help you establish which styles are most prevalent in your classroom.
Click the button below to see the full template in the AhaSlides editor.
Go through each question together, with each student answering on their phones.
Us too. They’re not very fun.
A scales slide lets students pick the extent to which they agree with a statement between 1 and 5.
Naturally, you’re going to get a much wider range of answers in an open-ended slide, but each answer can give you a clue as to which learning style best suits each student.
On multiple choice and scales slides, it’s only possible to see how all your students voted, not how each one voted. But, a simple workaround is to directly ask your students which answers they voted for in the previous set of questions.
This way, you have the name of each student and the overall responses they gave to statements. Statements and answers are always worded like this:
maths, art, graphic design, media studies or anything else involving symbols, images and patterns. Visual
(gym), music, chemistry or anything else with a focus on physical exploration. Kinaesthetic
What’s your favourite hobby outside of school?
Writing notes, making diagrams, memorising from textbooks… Visual
In short bursts, making flashcards, imagining stories… Kinaesthetic
While this data is intended for you, the teacher, we totally understand that you might want to share it with your students. Students can learn a lot about different learning styles through this assessment, and can get a better grasp of how they should tailor their own studying.
If your classroom has a projector or TV, simply hook up your laptop and students will be able to follow the live updates of the results. If you’re teaching online, you can share your laptop screen over the video conferencing software (Zoom, Microsoft Teams…) you’re using with your students.
It’s also possible to capture the final data of your assessment, export it and share it with your students:
Export to PDF – This is a single PDF file with images of each of your slides, as well as their response data.
