Want to make your own new year trivia?
Usually, the content for this kind of quiz revolves around events that happened in the previous year, but that’s not always the case. You may want to make a general knowledge quiz, or a best friend quiz to round off 2021, but that’s up to you.
If you want to create your own quiz, let’s start, as is traditional, with the first question….
You can choose to make a quiz entirely of multiple choice and/or open-ended questions, or you can choose to end the year with a bit of variety. The best quiz masters go for the latter.
Image questions – No fiddly materials and no admin. Just write the question on AhaSlides, provide 4 image options and let your players guess the right one.
Matching questions – Give your players a column of prompts and a column of answers. They must match the right prompt to the right answer.
With your question slide created, you can now go ahead and write your super engaging quiz question. You also need to provide the answer (or answers) that your players have to get to earn their points.
Once you choose your settings on the first slide, those settings will take affect on every slide you create aftwards. So, it’s a good idea to nail down your ideal settings right from the off, so that you can stay consistent throughout your quiz.
You’ll find a lot more settings in the ‘Quiz Settings’ menu in the top bar. Learn more about each setting here.
Creating the perfect quiz to round off the year is no easy task, but here are some golden guidelines to follow during the creation process…
Reward faster answers – In a great new year quiz, it’s not just about getting it right or wrong, it’s also about how fast you do so. AhaSlides gives you the option to reward faster answers with more points, which adds a real kick to the drama.
Keep it topical – The main theme of your new year’s quiz should be a roundup of the year. That means notable events, news stories, music and film releases, etc., NOT a quiz about the (fairly sparse) traditions of new year.
After you’ve made a bunch of new year quiz questions, it’s ready to go!
To do this, simply…
Enter the URL at the top of the screen into your phone.
Answer a quiz question and see what happens!
If everything goes to plan, you’ll be able to answer a question correctly and see your own points tally on the following leaderboard slide.
Join code – Give your players the unique URL link at the top any slide. A player can enter this into their phone browser to join your quiz.
Once they’re in, they’ll need to enter their name, choose an avatar, and if you’ve opted to run a team quiz, select the team that they want to be a part of.
Step 4: Host your New Year Quiz!
The competition starts here, so when you’ve got all your players waiting in the lobby, press ‘Start the quiz’.
Don’t stop talking – Quizzes are never meant to be a silent. Read each question aloud twice and have some interesting facts ready to mention while players are waiting for others to answer.
Keep it relaxed – Remember, this is all a bit of fun!
We hear it all the time: a great teacher is a great motivator. It’s a simple idea, but it rests on a concept that educators have been battling for decades: how do I motivate my students?
It’s a vicious cycle, but the 12 student engagement strategies below can help you to stop the rot.
You’ve taken the right step towards improving your students’ learning. Here’s why it makes so much sense to have student engagement strategies in the classroom:
By the end of the 2020 academic year, 1.3 million students had stopped engaging because of the switch to distanced learning. (Remind)
Disengagement itself is an epidemic, but there are always techniques to stop it. The tips below will help you rekindle your students innate curiosity to learn.
The four techniques below are the quickest and easiest ways to capture student interest. They require very little work to set up and they’re easily understandable for all levels of your students.
Polls are powerful, because polls connect your subject matter with the centre of any young person’s universe – themselves.
Giving them a participating voice in your lesson has numerous benefits, but none more so than letting students know that their opinion, not your subject matter, is the real star of the show here.
This poll works great for engagement because:
Students can instantly see how their opinion stacks up with others’ around them.
From a solid and diverse poll, strategy number 2 becomes the natural next step…
There’s one learner engagement strategy that’s more comprehensive than a poll. A full-blown discussion.
They can enter their answer anonymously, meaning there’s no fear of judgement for what they wrote. For the strong contingent of every class that has self-conscious students, the simplicity of anonymous answering can be an incredible boost to engagement.
We’ve got a full guide on how to hold a student debate in 6 steps!
Dr. Tom Verhoeff, Eindhoven University of Technology.
Well, if you’re anything like me, it’s a live quiz. By quizzes, I don’t mean exams or tests; I mean a proper quiz with a leaderboard, fun, drama and one hugely engaged set of participants.
Either solo or in teams, the rush of students competing against their peers can be a whirlwind of engagement. If stakes are high (i.e., the prize is good), quizzes can be one of the most effective student engagement techniques in this list.
Keep it to around 10 questions – Let your students get into it, but don’t let them tire of it.
They’re anonymous – Students can remain nameless and ask anything without fear.
They’re organised – All answers are written, can be sorted into different categories and remain permanent.
Get everything above, and more, for 100% free. Download the full student engagement template and get students participating live in class.
For a teacher alone, this is hard to overcome, but giving students the responsibility of teaching part of the subject matter is a fantastic remedy.
Cycle back to your own teacher training. Were you more engaged during textbook exercises on behavioural management, or when facing a sea of young faces during an observed practical?
Here are a few tips on turning students into teachers: Do it gradually.
Give them a brief time slot to teach, so as not to overwhelm them. When they’re teaching, keep an eye on the clock so that they understand that time is a crucial factor in teaching.
If you’re a kinaesthetic learner, you need more than a token practical activity thrown your way every week. Auditory learners need more than 2 discussions each semester.
Illustrating concepts, taking notes, watching videos, playing quizzes – (Visual)
Protip Define each student’s individual learning style with these 25 questions.
When I was teaching English in Vietnam, I noticed that all textbooks exclusively referenced British or American culture. According to the National Council of Teachers of English (NCTE), because my Vietnamese students found nothing relevant to their culture in lessons, they were much more likely to tune out.
Once you’ve got answers back from your students, you can shape explanations and exercises around their interests.
In the case of older students, there are two things that all activities need to have: relevance (which we just discussed) and choice.
Here are a few ways to incorporate choice into your classroom:
Structure – Lay out the structure of the lesson and let them choose how they want to proceed.
It’s best to introduce choice slowly into your lessons. Most students are so devoid of choice at school, and possibly in their lives, that they’re often not sure how it works in the classroom.
Check out this excellent account of how a teacher increased student focus through offering choice.
Online learning is becoming more and more widespread, but keeping students motivated across distances seems to be getting tougher and tougher. Here are 4 tips to try something different in your remote classroom.
When pretty much all lessons went online in 2020, there was an understandable tendency for teachers to stick with the offline methodology that they knew. That have flown in the early stages; it won’t fly now.
Here are a few free tools that teachers and students can use in online lessons:
An interactive presentation maker that allows students to host a topic and create live polls, quizzes and brainstorms about it.
A simple but powerful piece of photo editing and graphic design software. Colorcinch has a large collection of vector graphics, stock photos, and special effects.
A communal whiteboard that students can use to brainstorm, illustrate thinking processes and design solutions with each other at the same time.
In the remote schooling world, where schoolwork and homework are done at the same desk, flipped learning is more about swapping the roles of synchronous work (with the live teacher) and asynchronous work (without the live teacher).
Check out some of the benefits of flipped learning for increasing student participation:
More autonomy and freedom of ownership of their studies puts students in control – a hugely motivating factor.
Want to give it a go?
Before the lesson: Create a shared folder of topic material for students (videos, podcasts, taped lectures, reading resources, etc.) and tell them to progress through each material.
Check out this great introduction to flipped learning by Lesley University
How much more motivated would you be if you knew that your work was going to be shown to your peers?
Essentially, a gallery walk is a slideshow in which students’ works are displayed for each other to see. While viewing a piece of work, students make observations and note down their feelings on the piece.
It increases student motivation through their inherent sense of competition.
It increases student freedom of expression, which is always a positive for motivation.
At a time when students needed social interaction and collaboration the most, many teachers decided that translating group work to the online world was an impossible task. What resulted was students spending most of their ‘learning’ time feeling completely isolated from their classmates.
Give them access to file sharing software, like Google Drive.
Use ‘breakout rooms’ on Zoom and other video calling software to simulate real-world group work.
Get hands raised in every lesson.
