There are approximately 30 million PowerPoint presentations being given each day. PowerPoint has become such an essential part of a presentation that we can’t really fathom presenting without one.
In an extreme case, death by PowerPoint kills, literally. Most people use PowerPoint like a drunk uses a lamppost – for support rather than for illumination.
But how do you create a presentation that illuminates your audience and avoid death by PowerPoint?
Use dark background
In fact, the human brain can processes images 60,000 times faster than text, and 90 percent of information transmitted to the brain is visual. Therefore, fill your presentations with visual data to achieve maximum effect.
AhaSlides is a cloud-based interactive presentation software that sheds the static, linear approach to presenting. Not only does it offer a more visually dynamic flow of ideas, it also provides interactive elements keep your audience engaged.
Use multiple media to engage your audience
You can add a slide with your contact information on Twitter, Facebook, or LinkedIn at the beginning of your presentation.
Send out a light reading or play a fun ice breaker to create audience engagement. If your presentation involves abstract concepts or complex ideas, you can define them before hand so your audience would be on the same level as you when you present.
Handouts have gotten a bad rap, partly because of how dull and awfully long they usually are. But if you use them wisely, they can be your best friend in the presentation.
Visualizing your presentation with a prop. As mentioned above, some people are visual learners, so having a prop would enhance their experience with your presentation.
Using props might not be relevant to all the cases, but this example shows that sometimes using a physical object can be more impactful than any computer slide. Final Words
