Let’s not mince words here: Kahoot is a giant of the edtech world. It’s used by 50% of U.S teachers and in over 200 countries across the globe, with a big community and acclaim in many different spheres.
Here are 7 of the best alternatives to Kahoot!
As one of the top alternatives to Kahoot, we at AhaSlides have been helping ex-Kahoot users move over to the simpler, cheaper and safer side. Here’s some of what we hear those users complaining about the most….
Limited free plan Kahoot’s free plan allows for 2 question types – multiple choice and true or false. No flexibility in stuff like number of answers, multiple answer select, typed answers, etc.
Pricing is not just confusing, but for many plans it’s also really high. A one-off event will cost a minimum of $250 – that’s 85x more expensive than its competitors!
Inflexible plans For most plans on Kahoot!, you’ll be locked into an entire year’s subscription, as monthly plans are mostly not available. AhaSlides
Limited personalisation No way to make a Kahoot yours. No options to change the layout, background, colour or text on any slide.
Easy to hack Not really Kahoot’s fault, this one, but the worldwide use of the software leaves it open to sabotage. There are even full communities and websites set up to ruin live Kahoot games!
Limited customer support Email is the only channel with which to contact a human at Kahoot. The live chat is a rather apathetic robot.
AhaSlides is the all-in-one presentation, education and training tool. It’s a simple, easy-to-use and very much free alternative to Kahoot, with a no-pay plan that gives you all the freedom you need to host incredible interactive presentations.
More than a gaming tool, AhaSlides lets you gather opinions through polls, share ideas through brainstorms, make word clouds, hold Q&As, run live quizzes, and yes, play games (like the spinner wheel) as well.
The free plan is actually usable – While Kahoot’s free plan gives you very little to work with, AhaSlides’ lets you use pretty much all of its features straight off the bat. The main limitation of its free plan relates to the size of your audience, so if you have more than 7 participants, you’ll have to upgrade.
– Remember when we said Kahoot’s one-time plans are 85 times the price of its competitors?
There’s no mode to test a presentation – Before running a presentation, you’ll want to test it. While Kahoot has a specific test mode for this, on AhaSlides you’ll just have to join your own presentation on multiple other devices.
For gamification of teaching.
The bones of it is that GimKit is a very charming and fun way to get students engaged in lessons. The question offerings it provides are simple (just multiple choice and type answer), but it offers many inventive game modes and a virtual money-based scoring system to keep students coming back time and again.
If GimKit Live wasn’t enough, your $4.99 a month will also get you access to GimKit Ink, a tool to help your students share and discuss their work.
It’s super varied – The premise of GimKit Live is very simple, but the amount of game mode variations makes it difficult for students to get bored. It also lets students create their own questions for a ‘kit’ and it keeps competition levels high over long periods of time with its awesome ‘seasons’ mode.
Question types are limited – If you just want a simple quiz with multiple choice and open-ended questions, then GimKit Live will do. However, if you’re after ordering questions, ‘closest answer wins’ questions or mix and match questions, you’re best looking for another Kahoot alternative.
If you’re thinking of leaving Kahoot, but are worried about leaving that enormous library of amazing user-created quizzes behind, then you better check out Quizizz.
It’s very easy to use and the friction is minimal. For teachers without a lot of time on their hands, or really anyone wanting accessibility to monthly plans, Quizizz can be a top Kahoot alternative.
Great reports – The reports system is detailed and allows you to create flashcards out of questions on which participants didn’t answer so well.
No live support – Unfortunately, those fed up with Kahoot’s lack of live chat might feel likewise with Quizizz. Support is limited to email, Twitter and support tickets.
Reliability – For those with trust issues, you need not worry. Canvas is very vocal about their 99.99% uptime and prides itself on the fact that only the slightest of ultra-slight chances will cause the software to fail on you.
Community communication – Canvas have built a strong and active community of teachers, adminstrators and students. Many members are brand evangelists and will post religiously on the forum to help out fellow teachers.
The difference is that Slido focuses more on team meetings and training than education, games or quizzes. The love of images and colour that a lot of alternatives to Kahoot (including Kahoot) has is replaced in Slido by ergonomic functionality.
Integrates directly with Google Slides and PowerPoint – This means you can embed a bit of Slido-brand audience participation directly into your presentation.
Uniform greyness – By far the biggest con of Slido is that there’s very little room for creativity or vibrancy. Kahoot certainly doesn’t do much in terms of personalising colour or text, but it at least has more options than Slido.
Expensive one-timers – Also like Kahoot, one-time plans may just break the bank. $69 is the cheapest, while $649 is the most expensive.
When you boil Kahoot down to the bones, it’s mainly used as a way to test students rather than impart new knowledge on them. If that’s the way you use it, and you aren’t too concerned with the extra frills, then ClassMarker might be your perfect, free alternative to Kahoot!
While the basics are all available for free, there’s still a lot hiding behind the paywall. Analytics, certificates, the ability to upload images… it’s all stuff the modern educator might want, but it’s only available for a minimum of $19.95 a month.
Incredible variety – There’s the standard multiple choice, true or false and open-ended questions, but also matching pairs, grammar spotting and essay questions. There are even different types within those question types, as well as the chance to change the scoring system, add fake answers to throw students off the scent, and more.
Students need accounts – On the ClassMarker free version, you need to assign quizzes to ‘groups’, and the only way to make a group is to have all students within that group sign up to ClassMarker.
This software gives you decent variety when it comes to asking questions. Opinion polls, surveys, clickable images and even some (very) basic quiz facilities mean you can have lessons with the student at the centre, though it’s clear from the setup that Poll Everywhere is far more suited to the work environment than to schools.
The interface of Poll Everywhere.
Lenient free plan – As a free software like Kahoot, Poll Everywhere is rather generous with the freebies. Unlimited questions of all types and a maximum audience number of 25.
No new updates – It seems like the developers of Poll Everywhere have more-or-less given up on updating the service. Don’t expect any new developments if you sign up.
One access code – With Poll Everywhere, you don’t create a separate presentation with a separate join code for each lesson. You only get one join code (your username), so you have to constantly ‘active’ and ‘deactivate’ questions that you do or don’t want to appear.
Poll Everywhere
Call us biased, but we genuinely believe that AhaSlides is one of the best alternatives to Kahoot out there. It’s simpler, cheaper, more lenient, more customisable and it offers more assistance to presenters.
Access to image library Yes $240 annually
Multiple choice polls Yes $240 annually
Start engaging your audience in the classroom, meeting room and beyond.
