Of course, that means that there’s an app for it.
and Charades – Heads Up Game, all available on your phone’s app store.
All guests download Heads Up!
Each player takes turns to choose a category and hold the phone to their forehead (or up to their computer screen’s camera if they’re sitting far away).
If the player guesses the correct word or phrase from the clues, they tilt the phone down.
If you’re unfamiliar, Scattergories is a game in which you name something in a range of categories that begins with a specific letter. Some category and letter combinations are super tough, and that’s what separates the wheat from the chaff.
Choose the categories from the list (you can sign up for free to access more categories).
Vote at the end for whether or not other players’ answers should be accepted.
This virtual party game involves trying to guess the meaning of a word you’ve almost definitely never heard of, then voting for who else’s answer you think sounds the most correct. Points are awarded for guessing the word correctly and for having someone vote for your answer as the correct answer.
That way, the funniest proposed definitions of a word can rake in the gold.
Create an ‘open-ended’ slide on AhaSlides and write your Fictionary word in the ‘your question’ field.
In ‘other settings’, turn on ‘hide results’ (to prevent copying) and ‘limit time to answer’ (to add drama).
Create a ‘multiple choice’ slide afterwards with the title ‘whose answer do you think was right?’
What can be a better way to honour Jeopardy‘s legendary host Alex Trebek than with mass Jeopardy playing across virtual parties this year? Jeopardy Labs is a fantastic and completely free tool that helps bring Jeopardy boards to life.
Write 5 categories across the top.
On party day, divide your partygoers into teams and share your screen.
This is a game that’s totally replicable using a ‘word cloud’ slide on AhaSlides. This type of slide puts the most common answers to statements in larger text at the centre, while those valuable obscure answers are outlying in smaller text.
Choose to hide the results and limit the time to answer.
Award points to whoever wrote the least popular answer(s) (there is no ‘name’ field on a word cloud slide, so you will have to ask who wrote the winning answer(s) and hope for honesty!)
It involves guessing what a picture is from a close-up section of that picture. You can make this as easy or as hard as you want, as you choose the pictures as well as how zoomed their close-ups are.
Click the ‘add image’ icon and select your image.
Want to add a bit of audio stimulation to the proceedings?
It’s times like these that we really miss those little quirks from family, friends and co-workers. Well, Impression Soundbite gives you the chance to alleviate that feeling by poking fun at other people’s hilarious quirks or infuriating habits.
Note: We’ve got heaps more tips on setting up audio quizzes on AhaSlides.
Laziness Rating: – Can do it with your eyes closed
One of these tools is Sync Video, which allows you and your guests to watch the same YouTube video at exactly the same time. It’s free to use and doesn’t require sign-up; simply invite guests to your room, queue up the jingles and take it in turns to belt them out!
Let everyone queue up songs to sing along to.
This works best for seasonal virtual parties, like Christmas, where there’s a certain setlist of songs that everyone knows. Just write out the first part of the lyric, then invite your guests to fill out the second part with their hilarious alternative.
Limit the time to answer.
If you want to embed an audio file…
Use Audio Trimmer to cut out the part of the song that you want to use.
We’re all for creativity at AhaSlides. Here are 7 ideas for creative activities at your next virtual party.
The crux of it is that, before the party, each guest will create a hilarious, informative or shocking presentation on any topic they want. Once the party kicks off and everyone has acquired a suitable amount of Dutch courage, they present their presentation to their fellow partygoers.
When it’s party time, let each person share their screen and present their presentation.
Note: Google Slides is one of the best free tools for making presentations. If you want to make a Google Slides presentation interactive with all of the free features of AhaSlides, you can do that in 3 simple steps.
Throwing an image design competition based around a certain theme can really light the fire under your virtual party.
Canva – A free tool to create images from a large library of templates, backgrounds and elements.
We made the above image for our virtual Christmas party invitation competition, but you could use any theme for your own virtual party.
Get each person to reveal their design at the party.
Here’s one of the best virtual party ideas for kids – drawing a monster with the help of free online tools!
Draw a Monster involves using your desktop or phone to draw a creature with a number of limbs dependent on the roll of a dice. You can use Draw Chat to roll the dice, assign numbers to limbs and challenge your guests to draw the monster in the most creative way possible.
Create a new page for each guest in the bottom left corner.
Assign each dice roll to a different limb.
Take a vote on the best monster at the end.
Laziness Rating (if using Draw Chat) : – Like a quick stretch before a workout
You may have guessed already after the previous virtual party idea, but Draw Chat is also a great tool for Pictionary.
Still, Pictionary entered the online world like many other games in 2020. Draw Chat is a great tool to play it online for free, but there’s also the super cheap Drawful 2, which gives guests a huge range of crazy concepts to draw with their phones.
Send a few words from your list to each of your guests.
Invite your guests using the personal whiteboard link.
Enter your best guess for each other player’s drawing.
You can start by making up (or finding online) a list of activities and situations for your guests to act out. If you’re hosting a virtual party for the holidays, it’s great to have a list of seasonal prompts that fit well with the time of year.
Note: We made the above charades list for a virtual Thanksgiving party. You can download it for free below:
Get them to act out their list over video.
Well, Sheet Hot Masterpiece is a great virtual party idea for creatives, as it lets anyone turn a regularly dull spreadsheet into a magnificent work of art through use of colourful conditional formatting.
Laziness Rating: – Not the easiest, but certainly not the hardest
Being stuck in the house for the majority of 2020 might have given you a fresh perspective on your possessions. Maybe not: “I’ve got too much stuff”, but almost definitely: “if I stack up all those used coffee pods, it might look like a collapsed Thing from Fantastic Four”.
Take a vote on which is the best/most loyal/most hilarious movie recreation.
Don’t feel like your virtual party has to be all action all the time. Sometimes it’s nice to step away from the competition, the extroversion and the commotion to simply chill out in a relaxed online space.
There’s not a chance that a pandemic is going to change our affinity for drinking during the holidays. The proof is in the Christmas pudding: virtual beer and wine tasting sessions have soared in popularity.
Share the unique room code with your drinkers and get discussing and tasting!
Watch2Gether is a free tool that lets you watch videos with your guests online at the same time – without the threat of lag. It differs from Sync Video (which we mentioned earlier) in that it allows the syncing of videos on platforms other than YouTube, such as Vimeo, Dailymotion and Twitch.
Upload a video of your choosing (or by consensus vote) to the box at the top.
Tip #1: After the movie, you could hold a quiz on what happened to see who was paying attention!
Well, having a virtual cookie-off is a pretty good start. We’ve found a great recipe from Brit + Co for gingerbread cookies, which are super simple and use basic ingredients found in every household.
While the cookies are baking, decide on who will recreate which emojis.
Make a ‘multiple choice’ slide to vote for the best recreation.
Group origami is the very definition of low-key. As long as it’s easy enough, that is.
Again, you can share a video like the one below on Sync Video or Watch2Gether, which gives you the option to pause the video if anyone gets stuck.
Go through the video together. Pause and rewind if anyone gets stuck.
Like our virtual beer tasting idea, you can incorporate free software into your book club to collect and compare opinions across your group. We’ve made another free template for you, including a mixture of open-ended questions, opinion polls, slides and word clouds that give your guests loads of ways to have their say on the material.
Change anything you want about the presentation, including questions, backgrounds and slide types.
When it’s virtual party day, invite your guests to the presentation using the unique room code at the top.
Protip The above presentation is just a template – you can change any part of it without any registration. Consider adding more questions and using more slide types to get the full range of responses from your fellow readers.
Tip #2: Let your audience progress through the presentation at their own pace by selecting ‘audience take the lead’ in the ‘settings’ tab.
CardzMania is a free online tool that lets you play over 30 different card games with your guests. Simply select your game, alter the rules and invite your players with the room code.
Head to CardzMania and find the card game you want to play.
That’s when services like Tabletopia turned up. Tabletopia lets you play 1000+ board games for free, all with full licensing by the real heavyweights and plucky newcomers of the board game world.
Share the room code with your guests.
The digitising of the communal jigsaw in 2020 was a celebratory event for retired dads everywhere (and many, many other demographics!)
The best free, multiplayer jigsaw tool we’ve used online is epuzzle.info. It lets you choose from a huge library of puzzles, or even create your own, then invite your friends via the join code.
Press ‘create a table’ and share the URL link with your party guests.
Use the settings in the top-right corner to see each player’s contribution to the puzzle and to see the box image.
More Ideas for Virtual Parties, Events and Meetings
You’ll find even more virtual party ideas across our other articles. We’ve also got ideas for events that you can hold online as well as ones for teams of remote workers.
AhaSlides – Presentation, polling and quizzing software that’s fully interactive and cloud-based. Participate and play from anywhere in the world.
Sync Video – An online tool to sync YouTube videos for watching at the same time as your guests.
Canva – Online software that helps you design graphics and other images with heaps of templates and elements.
Cardzmania – A tool to play over 30 varieties of card games with your guests.
Epuzzle – A tool for assembling virtual jigsaws with friends, either casually or competitively.
It’s no surprise, in unparalleled times like these, that the business of online surveys is soaring. With so many questions to ask of so many people, it’s never been more important to wrap your head around ordinal scales.
To get a clearer picture, let’s look at one super straightforward and common example: how satisfied are you with our services?
Chances are that you’ve seen this type of ordinal scale example before. It’s used to measure customer satisfaction on a 5-point scale:
Naturally, companies can use a satisfaction ordinal scale to determine if they need to improve their service. If they’re consistently scoring low numbers (1s and 2s) then it means action is much more urgent than if they were scoring high numbers (4s and 5s).
Qualitative – Ordinal scales are qualitative because they focus on words that define a specific value. For example, people know what a satisfactory experience feels like, whereas it’s harder for them to define a ‘7 out of 10’ experience.
Of course, there are plenty of ordinal scale examples outside the satisfied/not satisfied response set. Let’s take a look at some of them….
A teacher testing their students on familiarity of a certain subject. This gives the teacher an idea of what level of prior knowledge about that subject can be assumed before deciding where to start teaching it.
Frequency ordinal scales are used to measure how often an activity is performed. They’re useful for judging active behaviours and where to start changing them.
A survey gathering information about the degree to which the public are following rules. The data can be used to ascertain how well or how poorly a public information campaign is performing.
Intensity ordinal scales usually test the strength of a feeling or experience. This is often a hard metric to measure as it relates to something more conceptual and subjective than what is usually measured in ordinal scales.
A medical establishment testing patients on their perceived levels of pain before and after treatment. The data can be used to determine the efficacy of a service or procedure.
Importance ordinal scales rate how non-essential or essential people find a product, service, sector, activity or pretty much anything to be. These, themselves, are important types of polling questions to ask, as results can often be surprising.
A restaurant asking customers to put forward what is most important to them. Data from here can be used to figure out what parts of service need the most attention from management.
Agreement ordinal scales help to determine to what degree a person disagrees or agrees with a statement. These are some of the most widely used ordinal scale examples out there, as they can be used with any statement that you want a specific answer to.
A company surveying their customers about the usability of their website. They can make specific statements about what the company itself thinks and then see if their users agree or disagree with those statements.
Again, this is a widely used example of an ordinal scale, as ‘satisfaction’ is the ultimate goal of businesses. All parts of a survey, in one way or another, try to gather information on satisfaction about a service, but satisfaction ordinal scales do this overtly and obviously.
A university gathering levels of satisfaction about their enrollment service. The data can help them determine what most needs improving for potential future students.
A company gathering customer reviews of each aspect of their purchase and delivery. They can use the data to see where the customers are placing high expectations and where the company is failing to meet them.
Likelihood ordinal scales are a great way to figure out how likely or unlikely a person is going to take a mentioned action in the future. This is often after certain conditions have been met, such as when a transaction or a medical procedure is completed.
A company trying to determine what percentage of their customers will become advocates of the brand after using the service. This will reveal information that can help build brand loyalty across multiple channels.
Improvement ordinal scales provide a metric on progress across a certain time period. They measure an individual’s perception of to what degree the state of affairs has worsened or improved after a change has been implemented.
A company asking for employers’ opinions about which departments have worsened or improved in the past year. This will help them to make more meaningful efforts towards progress in certain areas.
Self-ability ordinal scales can be very interesting. They measure someone’s perceived level of competence at a certain task, which means that they can vary wildly depending on the level of self-esteem that different respondents in a group have.
A language teacher trying to determine how confident their students are in certain areas of language ability. The teacher can do this either before or after a lesson or course to determine improvement in self-perceived ability over time.
Usually when we talk about ordinal scales, we talk about them in the same breath as the Four Scales of Measurement, which are:
Here’s an example: I’m collecting some simple research data on hair colour. If I’m using a nominal scale, the values will simply be different hair colours (brown, blonde, black, etc.)
Here’s a nominal scale example about hair colour:
In this way, the ordinal scale example is giving us extra information. Not only does it reveal how many respondents of each hair colour we have (you can hover the mouse over any circular point to see how many responses it got), but we can also see the lightness or darkness of those hair colours on an 5-point scale between ‘super light’ (1) and ‘super dark’ (5).
And what value does a person with no hair choose?
One way is to leave a message for respondents that eliminates the chance of messing up the values:
So, let’s say I’m doing more simple research, this time into people’s ideal temperature at home and on holiday. In an ordinal scale format, I would set it out my values like this:
The big problem with this ordinal scale example is that it’s completely subjective. What’s considered ‘freezing’ for someone might be considered ‘temperate’ for someone else.
Instead, I should use an interval scale, which will name the exact degrees in Celsius or Farenheit that correspond to each value, like so:
Setting out the values this way means that my respondents can make their decisions based on an existing and well-known scaling system, rather than the biased perceptions of whoever wrote the question.
Doing this means that results are bound to be more varied and accurate, like this:
For example, take a look at this ratio scale on work experience:
Remember: not all zero values are ‘true zero’. The 0°C value from our interval scale is not a true zero because 0°C is a specific temperature, not the absence of temperature.
Don’t get us wrong here, ordinal scales really are great. But to make a truly engaging survey in the fields of education, work, politics, psychology or anything else, you’re going to want to branch out the format.
Image choice polls work much the same way as multiple choice polls, just more visual!
