The main purpose of the shock absorber is to limit the overall movement or sway of the car body. When the vehicle is running, the body will move up and down or left and right to varying degrees according to the driving and road conditions.
According to road conditions or driving conditions, the vehicle can change from smooth and controlled to bumpy and unstable in a short period of time. The shock absorber stabilizes the overall driving of the vehicle and prevents the vehicle from excessively tilting or rolling in any direction, especially when turning or making sharp turns.
In addition to controlling body movement and driving, the shock absorber exerts a huge stabilizing force on each vehicle tire. Shock absorbers can prevent vehicle tires from bouncing off on uneven roads and help vehicle tires to be firmly fixed on the ground or road surface.
By helping to stabilize and control the movements of a vehicle tire, a shock absorber helps to minimize tire tread wear. Tires that are held firmly against the ground and held in position by a firm shock absorber last longer and experience much less tread wear.
Shock absorbers are only part of a vehicle ’ s over all suspension system. Most vehicles use a combination of various leaf springs, coil springs and struts to stabilize and control the movement of the vehicle , reduce frame and body vibration rapidly .
