The first rubber molding process, rubber compression molding, is ideal for low to medium volume production of rubber products. Compression molding is a widely used economical production method for low volume production of medium to large parts.
The combination of elevated temperatures and high-pressure activates the vulcanization process and curing of the rubber compound. Once an optimal cure is reached, the part hardens and cools then the mold is opened and the final part removed.
This an ideal process for bonding rubber to metal and plastic parts, an example of such parts would be gears, shafts, rollers, bumpers, and stops in a wide array of sizes and shapes. This process is also useful for bonding rubber components to steel, aluminum, brass and plastic.
Additionally, rubber bonded to metal ranges in parts and in size from small inserts to very large components. Over molded rubber metal parts are applicable across a broad range of industries and applications.
