And while some ports are done well and appreciated by fans, others tend to not go over so well – leading to some seriously disappointed gamers.
But first, let’s explore some of the relevance, necessity, and benefits of porting games to the Switch.
As mentioned before, with 61.5 million Nintendo Switch consoles in living rooms and bedrooms around the world, it’s no wonder that game developers are interested in learning “how to port the game to Nintendo switch” successfully. The Switch offers gamers a level of convenience that other consoles just can’t provide – being able to play console-quality games on the go is a major selling point for the Switch.
However, there is one downside to Nintendo Switch porting that both gamers and developers need to be aware of – the limited hardware of the Switch means modern state-of-the-art games often need to be toned down to run smoothly on the Switch. This can often lead to a sub-par gaming experience that leaves fans feeling disappointed.
The first step in ensuring a smooth port is to optimize the game’s graphics for the Switch’s GPU. The process of porting a game often includes reworking the game’s graphics and making sure that they are suitable for the Switch’s limited hardware.
When porting a console game to the Nintendo Switch, developers need to make sure that the game is compatible with the Switch’s hardware and software. The process of porting a game often includes making changes to the game’s code to ensure compatibility with the Switch.
Engine and SDK updates
Finally, it is important to test and assess the game’s UX during the porting process. Where necessary, developers may need to make changes to the game’s UI and UX in order to ensure a smooth and enjoyable experience for Switch users.
What made the port of Skyrim so successful was the fact that it was handled by a team of experienced developers who knew how to optimize the game for the Switch’s limited hardware.And the port of Skyrim was tested and assessed thoroughly, resulting in a smooth and enjoyable gaming experience for Switch users. In contrast, the Nintendo Switch port of FIFA (2016) was not as well-received by fans of the franchise.
The developers of the FIFA port should have focused on optimizing the best part of the game, The Journey, for the Switch’s limited hardware. This way they might just have produced the holy grail of a portable football video game that looks great plays fantastically and you can take it anywhere!
Now, you might be asking yourself, how much does it cost to port a game to Nintendo Switch?
