The Mayan number system draws a lot of attention because it was very complete and developed, despite having been developed independently. This civilization had a clear notion of zero, something the Europeans did not have until the Hindus showed it to them.
There is not much information about the Mayan writing because Spanish priests ordered to burn all Mayan books.
Likewise, the numerical and mathematical system of the Maya was the first to develop a positional system. That is, the value of a digit or a number depended on its position.
This is because there is only three symbols, although the forms may vary depending on the use that is given to them. Some numberings were for the codices, others for the monuments and others even had human representations.
By combining these three symbols, the Mayan numbers from 0 to 20 could be obtained. From here on, it is important that you keep in mind that in the Mayan numeration quantities are grouped 20 by 20.
It is here where you can appreciate the positional system of the Mayans, in which the value of a number or figure varies depending on the position in which it is found, depending on the vertical position that the number occupies.
For example, in the number 25: in the lower part is 5 (the line that is equivalent to 5), and the upper part is equivalent to 20 (the point is equal to 1, but being in the upper part it is multiplied by 20) .
The Mayan numbering system we are talking about is the main and best known, but it is not the only numbering system used by the Mayans.
This other numbering system that they used is very characteristic because they used the heads of different deities to represent numbers, which is why it is known as the head numbering system. It is also a vigesimal system and its main number is 20.
They placed the Mayan numbers from 10 to 4 on the lower part of the chin of the deity that represented 9.
The Mayans were one of the most prodigious and amazing civilizations in the world, possibly the most advanced for their time in many ways. Its advances in mathematics, its numbering system, its calendar, its architecture, its knowledge of the universe, etc., in most of these respects surpassed any other contemporary civilization.
Historians continue to search for clues to his disappearance. At present, some of the hypotheses that are had about the abandonment of the Mayan cities speak about natural disasters, attacks by more powerful empires or even a depletion of resources that forced them to emigrate to places with more fertile lands.
All this knowledge was vastly superior to that of contemporary Europe and perhaps the world.
